            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Why Does the Credited Amount Always Differ from the Top-Up Amount?</h2>
<p>This is one of the most common concerns. The gap usually comes from two sources: the exchange rate applied by the payment channel or bank, which is not the real-time market rate, and platform handling fees on certain payment methods, such as a 0.5% service charge on credit card top-ups. If you top up $1,000 and receive approximately $993, this is normal and no repeated verification is needed.</p>
<h2>What to Do When Platform Spend Differs from Invoice Amount?</h2>
<p>Most platforms issue invoices after the billing cycle closes, and spend data from the platform dashboard should match the invoice. Common reasons for discrepancies include: Credit is applied first, which may be listed separately on the invoice; refunds or account adjustments are reflected in the next billing cycle rather than the current one. It is recommended to export platform spend details monthly and compare them line by line with the invoice, focusing on Credit Applied and Adjustment lines.</p>
<h2>How to Reconcile Credit Usage in Invoices?</h2>
<p>Credit consumption logic varies across platforms. Some platforms list Credit as a separate asset with “Credit Applied” shown on the invoice; others deduct Credit directly from the final payable amount without a separate line item. It is advisable to maintain a separate Credit ledger in your accounting system, tracking acquisition date, amount, validity period, and usage progress to avoid cross-month consumption logic errors.</p>
<h2>Any Efficient Methods for Multi-Platform, Multi-Account Reconciliation?</h2>
<p>Manually exporting Excel and consolidating becomes inefficient once you manage more than five accounts. We recommend building a unified reconciliation template with core fields: top-up date, top-up amount (original currency), platform exchange rate, credited amount, spend amount, Credit changes, invoice amount, and variance. Sync platform dashboard data weekly and use invoices as the final settlement reference for monthly financial reconciliation. If using multi-account management tools, prioritize those supporting automatic spend report exports to reduce manual work.</p>
<h2>Will the Platform Refund if Invoice Exceeds Actual Spend?</h2>
<p>Platforms typically do not proactively issue refunds. If the invoice exceeds actual spend, it is usually because the invoice was issued based on estimated consumption, with the difference offset in the next billing cycle. Another scenario is that platforms may charge account maintenance fees or forfeit unused balances for long-idle accounts. Therefore, it is advisable to avoid large advance top-ups and instead top up on a monthly or spend-driven basis to keep the account balance aligned with average monthly spend.</p>
<h2>How to Quickly Locate Discrepancies During Month-End Reconciliation?</h2>
<p>Follow a three-step logic: verify top-up records first, then consumption data, and finally invoices. First, confirm whether original differences come from exchange rate gaps or fees by comparing top-up records with credited amounts. Next, check whether the consumption period aligns with the billing period—for example, some platforms bill in UTC time, which may cause cross-day spend attribution differences for users in other time zones. Finally, verify whether Credit, coupons, or refunds are missing from the current invoice. A variance within 1% can generally be treated as a reasonable error and carried forward; discrepancies exceeding 2% require submitting a reconciliation dispute to the platform.</p>
